# What never enters the build context.
#
# BuildKit reads `<dockerfile>.dockerignore` in preference to the context root's
# `.dockerignore`, so this file belongs to this Dockerfile alone and no other
# build in the repository is affected by it.
#
# Two things are being kept out. The large working directories — a virtual
# environment, the fetched weights, the upstream drift clone — would be uploaded
# to the daemon on every build and are rebuilt inside it in any case. And
# anything carrying local values: `.env` is gitignored precisely because it holds
# an environment's real settings, and a build that copied one would bake it into
# a public image.

# Version control and local tooling state.
.git
.github
.claude
**/.mypy_cache
**/.pytest_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]

# Working directories that are rebuilt rather than carried.
.venv
**/.venv
.models
.upstream-drift
.duvet/reports
.duvet/requirements

# Coverage output.
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov

# Local values. The tracked `.example` sibling documents the shape; the real
# file never leaves the host it was written on.
#
# The patterns are nesting-aware, and that is the point rather than tidiness: a
# pattern matches against the path relative to the context root, so a bare
# `.env` would exclude one at the repository root and let
# `services/groundstation/deploy/.env` — the one the deployment instructions
# tell an operator to create, holding a real credential — into the context and
# onto a builder-stage layer.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
provisioning/ansible/inventory.ini
